---

<Base
  lang={lang}
  roomId="zil"
  title="ZIL and SLOG · Uberblok"
  description="How OpenZFS 2.4.4 makes fsync() return before the txg syncs: a per-dataset intent log of records, written to a chain of log blocks on a SLOG or on the pool, freed when the txg syncs and replayed after a crash. Interactive, with the code."
>
  <article class="wrap">
    <header class="room-head">
      <p class="kicker">Room {roomNumber(r.id)}</p>
      <h1>{r.title.en}</h1>
      <div class="prose problem">
        <p class="lead">
          A program that calls <code>fsync()</code> asks for one promise: when the call returns, its data survives a power
          cut. A database asks for it on every commit.
        </p>
        <p>
          ZFS writes its changes in <a href={roomUrl(lang, "txg")}>transaction groups</a>, and a txg is synced only every few
          seconds. Forcing a txg sync for each <code>fsync()</code> would rewrite large parts of the tree for a few bytes of
          data, and make the caller wait for all of it. ZFS does something else. Each dataset keeps an <em>intent log</em>,
          the ZIL: every change is noted as a small record, and on <code>fsync()</code> the records are written at once to a
          chain of log blocks. Once the txg holding the real change has synced, they are no longer needed and are thrown away.
          The log is read only after a crash. A separate log device, a <em>SLOG</em>, gives those blocks a home of their own.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="lab">
      <h2 id="lab"><span class="n">1</span> Write, fsync, cut the power</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Below, a fresh pool and one file, already several records long, whose blocks are 128 KiB; txg 100 is open, and the
          dataset has no log yet. Choose a log device,
          <code>logbias</code>, how the file is opened and a size: the first panel says what the code will decide for the
          next <code>write()</code>, and why. <code>write()</code> leaves a record in memory; <code>fsync()</code> writes it
          to the log, block by block, and returns once the disk has flushed. Sync the txg and the log blocks are freed. Cut
          the power whenever you like, then import the pool and mount the dataset.
        </p>
        <p>
          The first <code>fsync()</code> creates the log, and that first commit waits for the txg to sync, so it logs
          nothing; from the second on, records go into the log. So start with a write and <code>fsync()</code>, then try a
          4 KiB write and <code>fsync()</code> without a SLOG, then cut the power; the same with a sync before the cut; 128 KiB
          with and without a SLOG; <code>logbias=throughput</code>; a write without <code>fsync()</code> before the cut.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ZilLab lang={lang} />
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="depths">
      <h2 id="depths">The idea, at three depths</h2>

      <div class="depth">
        <h3>In one sentence</h3>
        <p class="lead prose">
          ZFS makes <code>fsync()</code> return early by writing at once only a record of the change, to a log kept per
          dataset, and the change itself later with its txg; the log is thrown away once that txg has synced, and read only
          after a crash.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="depth">
        <h3>In one paragraph</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          Every <code>write()</code> makes an in-memory record, an <em>itx</em>, under the open txg.
          <code>fsync()</code>, or a write to a file opened with <code>O_DSYNC</code>, commits the file’s records: they are
          packed into <em>log write blocks</em>, lwbs, each written as one block whose trailer points to the next block, so
          the log is a chain; the call returns when the block holding its last record is written and the disk caches are
          flushed. How much of the data goes into the log is decided write by write: copied into the record
          (WR_COPIED), copied when the block is written (WR_NEED_COPY), or written in place in the pool with the record
          only pointing to it (WR_INDIRECT), so that large data is not written twice. With a SLOG, unless
          <code>logbias=throughput</code> or Direct I/O, the data always goes into the log. The log’s blocks come from the SLOG first; a pool without one takes them from space reserved on its
          ordinary vdevs. When a txg syncs, the log blocks whose records all belong to synced txgs are freed. After a crash,
          importing the pool claims the chain, and mounting the dataset replays every record whose txg had not synced.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="depth">
        <h3>In detail</h3>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            <strong>A record per write.</strong> In <code>zfs_write</code>, every write is logged by
            <a href={L.logWrite}><code>zfs_log_write</code></a>, which makes an itx and <a href={L.itxAssign}>queues it</a>
            under the write’s txg, <a href={L.lrcTxg}><code>lrc_txg</code></a>. Nothing is written yet. If the file was
            opened with <code>O_SYNC</code> or <code>O_DSYNC</code>, or the dataset has <code>sync=always</code>, the write
            is <a href={L.commitFlag}>committed at once</a>: <code>zfs_write</code> <a href={L.writeCommit}>calls
            <code>zil_commit</code></a> before it returns. Otherwise the record waits for <code>fsync()</code>, which
            <a href={L.fsync}>calls the same function</a>. What the record holds is decided first:
          </p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={Z}
          from={2212}
          to={2246}
          symbol="zil_write_state"
          notes={[
            {
              from: 2216,
              to: 2217,
              text: `<code>logbias=throughput</code>, or a write that took the <a href="${L.direct}">Direct I/O path</a>: always indirect, SLOG or not. The log block that holds the record still <a href="${L.slogFirst}">comes from the SLOG first</a>: <code>logbias</code> decides where the data goes, not the log.`,
            },
            {
              from: 2219,
              to: 2228,
              text: `Indirect only from <a href="${L.immediate}">32 KiB</a>, and, for a write committed at once, only from half a block, 64 KiB for the figure’s file, because, as the comment above says, the next write might rewrite the same block.`,
            },
            {
              from: 2230,
              to: 2232,
              text: `A pool with a SLOG never logs indirect. <a href="${L.hasSlogs}"><code>spa_has_slogs</code></a> asks whether the log class has a device, not whether it has room: a full SLOG still counts.`,
            },
            {
              from: 2233,
              to: 2238,
              text: `Special vdevs without a SLOG: indirect only when the file’s blocks go to the special class (or <a href="${L.specialIsSlog}"><code>zil_special_is_slog</code></a> is 0). The figure’s pool has no special vdev.`,
            },
            {
              from: 2240,
              to: 2245,
              text: `Indirect: the record carries a pointer. Otherwise WR_COPIED, the data copied into the record now, when the write is committed at once; or WR_NEED_COPY, the data <a href="${L.getData}">read from the file</a> when the log block is filled.`,
            },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            Then <code>zfs_log_write</code> <a href={L.downgrade}>turns WR_COPIED into WR_NEED_COPY</a> when the data is over
            <a href={L.maxCopied}>7680 bytes</a>, since a copied record must fit in one log block, and
            <a href={L.oneBlock}>cuts an indirect write at each file block</a>. <code>O_DIRECT</code> takes the Direct I/O path only for a write of at least a file block: <a href={L.shortDirect}>a shorter one goes through the ARC</a>, so in the figure only the 128 KiB write with <code>O_DIRECT</code> is Direct I/O. For WR_INDIRECT, the comment of
            <code>zfs_get_data</code> gives the reason: <a href={L.twice}>“for large writes it’s cheaper to sync the data and
            get a pointer to it (indirect) so that we don’t have to write the data twice”</a>. The block is written in place
            <a href={L.dmuSync}>by <code>dmu_sync</code></a>, or was already written by the
            <a href={L.directBp}>Direct I/O write</a>. In the figure, the panel at the top runs this function for every
            combination of the buttons; the badge on each write is its result.
          </p>
          <p><strong>Committing.</strong> <code>zil_commit_impl</code>, called by <code>fsync()</code>:</p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={Z}
          from={4097}
          to={4122}
          symbol="zil_commit_impl"
          notes={[
            {
              from: 4097,
              to: 4098,
              text: `A <em>commit waiter</em> is queued as a special itx, behind every record of the file (the file’s other records were <a href="${L.async}">moved to the sync list</a> just before).`,
            },
            { from: 4100, text: "The pending records are packed into lwbs and the lwbs issued." },
            {
              from: 4101,
              text: `The caller sleeps until the lwb holding its waiter is written and flushed (<a href="${L.waiterDone}"><code>zil_commit_waiter_done</code></a>).`,
            },
            {
              from: 4104,
              to: 4117,
              text: "On a log write error, fall back to waiting for a full txg sync: just as durable, only slower. The figure has no I/O error.",
            },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p><strong>An lwb’s life.</strong> Each lwb goes through seven states, in this order:</p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path="include/sys/zil_impl.h"
          from={83}
          to={92}
          symbol="lwb_state_t"
          notes={[
            { from: 84, to: 85, text: `New, then <a href="${L.open}">opened</a> on its first record.` },
            {
              from: 86,
              text: `<a href="${L.close}">Closed</a> when full, or at the end of a commit, which also <a href="${L.nextLwb}">creates the next lwb</a>, still empty.`,
            },
            {
              from: 87,
              to: 88,
              text: `<a href="${L.ready}">Ready</a> when filled with its data; <a href="${L.issued}">issued</a> once the next block is allocated.`,
            },
            {
              from: 89,
              to: 90,
              text: `<a href="${L.writeDone}">Write done</a>, then <a href="${L.flushDone}">flush done</a> when the disk caches are flushed: the waiters on it <a href="${L.waiters}">are woken</a> and <code>fsync()</code> returns.`,
            },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            The comment above the enum <a href={L.stateComment}>describes the transitions</a> and the locks they need. With
            one writer and nothing else in flight, as in the figure, the lwb holding a commit is
            <a href={L.closeAtOnce}>closed and issued at once</a> rather than kept open for more records. The figure draws
            each state on each lwb, in the memory panel.
          </p>
          <p><strong>Packing.</strong> How records go into lwbs:</p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={Z}
          from={2334}
          to={2369}
          symbol="zil_lwb_assign"
          notes={[
            {
              from: 2334,
              to: 2346,
              text: "A record that does not fit closes the lwb and opens the next. The long test lets a WR_NEED_COPY record start in an almost full block only when that saves a split.",
            },
            { from: 2348, to: 2357, text: "A WR_COPIED record must fit whole: it is never split." },
            {
              from: 2359,
              to: 2369,
              text: "A WR_NEED_COPY record larger than the room left is split: a clone takes what fits, the rest goes on to the next block.",
            },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            Each record then gets the next sequence number, <a href={L.seq}><code>lrc_seq</code></a>, and the lwb keeps
            <a href={L.addTxg}>the highest txg of its records</a>. A write record’s header, <a href={L.lrWrite}><code>lr_write_t</code></a>,
            is 192 bytes; a log block’s trailer, <a href={L.chainT}><code>zil_chain_t</code></a>, 184 bytes; a log block at
            most <a href={L.maxBlock}>128 KiB</a>. So a 128 KiB block holds 130,888 bytes of records, and a 128 KiB WR_NEED_COPY write
            is <a href={L.maxLogData}>130,696 bytes</a> in one lwb and 376 bytes in the next: the figure shows it with a SLOG.
          </p>
          <p><strong>The chain, and where the SLOG comes in.</strong> When an lwb is issued, it allocates the block of the <em>next</em> lwb:</p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={Z}
          from={2073}
          to={2092}
          symbol="zil_lwb_write_issue"
          notes={[
            {
              from: 2073,
              to: 2074,
              text: `The next block is allocated now, and its pointer goes into this block’s trailer, <a href="${L.nextBlk}"><code>zc_next_blk</code></a>: each block points to the next, from <code>zh_log</code> in the header on.`,
            },
            { from: 2087, text: "The new block is born in an open txg." },
            {
              from: 2088,
              to: 2091,
              text: "It inherits this block’s checksum seed, with the sequence word incremented: at replay, that is how a valid next block is told from old data.",
            },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            The first block of a chain, <a href={L.minBlk}>4 KiB</a>, comes from <a href={L.create}><code>zil_create</code></a>,
            through the same allocator, <code>zio_alloc_zil</code>:
          </p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path="module/zfs/zio.c"
          from={4561}
          to={4595}
          symbol="zio_alloc_zil"
          notes={[
            {
              from: 4561,
              to: 4565,
              text: `The log class, the SLOG devices, first. Success sets <code>slog</code>, which becomes <a href="${L.slogFlag}"><code>LWB_FLAG_SLOG</code></a> on the lwb.`,
            },
            { from: 4567, to: 4580, text: "Then log space reserved on special vdevs, and the special class." },
            {
              from: 4582,
              to: 4587,
              text: `Then the <em>embedded log</em>: space every ordinary top-level vdev <a href="${L.vdevLogMg}">reserves for the ZIL</a>. A pool without a SLOG, or with a full one, and without special vdevs, logs there.`,
            },
            { from: 4589, to: 4595, text: "Last, the normal class." },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            <strong>The first commit waits for a txg.</strong> <code>zil_create</code> is called by the commit
            <a href={L.createCall}>when the dataset has no log</a>. It cannot write the new block into <code>zh_log</code>
            itself: the header changes only in syncing context. So it allocates the block in a transaction and
            <a href={L.createWait}>waits for that txg to sync</a>. The writes of that txg are then in the tree, so the commit
            <a href={L.synced}>finds their txg synced</a> and <a href={L.dropSynced}>destroys their itxs</a> instead of logging
            them; only the commit waiter goes into the first lwb. The first commit on a new log, an <code>fsync()</code> or an
            <code>O_DSYNC</code> write, therefore costs a txg sync, and that is <a href={L.keepWhy}>why a log with nothing to replay keeps its first block</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            In the figure, every allocation lists the classes it tried. The code says where a log block goes, not how fast the
            device is: what a SLOG buys depends on the device.
          </p>
          <p><strong>When the txg syncs.</strong> In syncing context, for each dataset whose log changed:</p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={Z}
          from={4206}
          to={4224}
          symbol="zil_sync"
          notes={[
            { from: 4207, text: "The header’s <code>zh_log</code> follows the head of the list." },
            {
              from: 4208,
              to: 4210,
              text: "An lwb is freed once it is flushed, every record in it belongs to a synced txg, and its own block was allocated in this txg or earlier. The first that is not stops the loop.",
            },
            { from: 4211, to: 4214, text: "Its block goes back to the allocator." },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            The records of a synced txg that were never written to the log are simply dropped by
            <a href={L.clean}><code>zil_clean</code></a>: the txg made them durable. In the figure, freed blocks are greyed
            out and crossed through.
          </p>
          <p><strong>After a crash: claim.</strong> At import, in the pool’s <a href={L.spaClaim}>first txg</a>, for each dataset:</p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={Z}
          from={1232}
          to={1253}
          symbol="zil_claim"
          notes={[
            {
              from: 1239,
              to: 1241,
              text: `<a href="${L.parse}"><code>zil_parse</code></a> walks the chain from <code>zh_log</code>, claiming each block so that the allocator does not hand it out again, and <a href="${L.claimWrite}">the blocks WR_INDIRECT records point to</a>. The walk ends at the block allocated but never written: its checksum does not match.`,
            },
            { from: 1242, text: "The claim txg: the pool’s first txg." },
            {
              from: 1245,
              to: 1246,
              text: `<a href="${L.flags}"><code>ZIL_REPLAY_NEEDED</code></a> if a record was found, or more than one block followed. The walk counts the unwritten last block, so a log with nothing in it counts one.`,
            },
            { from: 1253, text: "That first txg is the last synced txg plus one." },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p><strong>Replay.</strong> At mount, <a href={L.mount}><code>zil_replay</code></a> runs each record through:</p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={Z}
          from={4696}
          to={4712}
          symbol="zil_replay_log_record"
          notes={[
            { from: 4708, to: 4709, text: "A record already replayed by an earlier, interrupted replay is skipped." },
            {
              from: 4711,
              to: 4712,
              text: "A record of a txg older than the claim txg is skipped: that txg synced, the change is already in the tree. Every other record is applied again.",
            },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            Without <code>ZIL_REPLAY_NEEDED</code>, <code>zil_replay</code> <a href={L.noReplay}>only destroys the log</a>,
            keeping its first block: the emptied header <a href={L.keepFirst}>names it again</a>, under a new chain GUID, and
            the next commit <a href={L.reuse}>writes there</a> without allocating. Otherwise it replays, then
            <a href={L.destroy}>destroys the log</a>. The header that holds all this,
            <a href={L.header}><code>zil_header_t</code></a>, lives in the dataset: <code>zh_claim_txg</code>,
            <code>zh_replay_seq</code>, <code>zh_log</code>, the claimed sequence numbers and the flags. So a crash before the
            sync brings back every record of the unsynced txg; a crash after it finds a log with nothing to replay, and a
            write that was never <code>fsync()</code>ed and never synced is lost, as its program allowed. After a replay the
            whole log is destroyed, so the next commit creates a new one and waits for a txg again.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section simplified" aria-labelledby="simplified">
      <h2 id="simplified">What the figure leaves out</h2>
      <p class="prose muted">
        The figure runs the functions above on one file of one dataset, with one writer and one txg in flight, synced at once
        in one pass. The file already spans several records, so its blocks are 128 KiB; a file of a single block smaller
        than <code>recordsize</code> <a href={L.blksz}>has a smaller block</a>, and the half-block rule uses it. The first block of a new chain is 4 KiB, as in the code; every later log block is
        128 KiB, where the code chooses each block’s size from the burst of records
        (<a href={L.plan}><code>zil_lwb_plan</code></a>). So in a real chain records split at other places, and the byte
        counts in the figure are those of 128 KiB blocks. Each <code>write()</code> starts at the beginning of a file block.
        There is no I/O error, so no <a href={L.fallback}>fallback to <code>txg_wait_synced</code></a>; the flush is one step
        for the whole lwb, without the per-vdev flush tree; the commit timeout never fires, because one writer closes its lwb
        at once. Writes over <a href={L.bulk}><code>zil_slog_bulk</code></a> per commit, 64 MiB, go to a SLOG at
        <a href={L.bulkUse}>a lower priority</a>; the Slim ZIL layout and encryption are not drawn. None of these changes a
        write state, a class, or which writes are replayed. When a txg syncs, see <a href={roomUrl(lang, "txg")}>Transaction groups</a>;
        how the classes share the pool’s space, <a href={roomUrl(lang, "metaslabs")}>Metaslabs and space maps</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
  <RoomNav lang={lang} roomId="zil" />
</Base>

